Pending UDP queue — park datagrams awaiting ARP resolution
==========================================================

Two-slot queue holding UDP datagrams whose next-hop MAC isn't
in the ARP cache yet. `inet_send_udp` parks the payload here
and fires the ARP request instead of dropping; when the reply
arrives, `inet_arp_drain_pending` retries each matching entry.
This removes the "retry sendto a few times with a sleep" dance
that every UDP client would otherwise need on cold start.

Bounded queue size keeps memory usage predictable in a
single-threaded server; queue-full callers fall back to
drop-on-miss semantics. Phase 2 chunk 3 added per-tid cleanup
so a dying process doesn't have its parked datagrams sent on
its behalf once an ARP reply finally lands.

Same `oskit.servers` module as `inet_proto.lsysl`, so siblings
(`inet_arp_lookup`, `inet_resolve_mac`, `inet_send_udp`,
`inet_send_arp_request`, `inet_tcp_arp_drain`) resolve without
imports.

    module oskit.servers

    import std.alloc.{free}
    import std.net.{ipv4}


### Pending UDP queue

Two-slot queue holding UDP datagrams whose next-hop MAC isn't
in the ARP cache yet. `inet_send_udp` parks the payload here
and fires the ARP request instead of dropping; when the reply
arrives, `inet_arp_drain_pending` retries each matching entry.
This removes the "retry sendto a few times with a sleep" dance
that every UDP client would otherwise need on cold start.

    val INET_PENDING_UDP_MAX = 2
    val INET_PENDING_UDP_DATA_MAX = 1472

    struct InetPendingUdp
        in_use: int
        owner_tid: int
        dst_ip: u32
        dst_port: int
        src_port: int
        dlen: int
        data: [1472]byte

    var inet_pending_udp: [2]InetPendingUdp


Park (dst_ip, dst_port, src_port, data[0..dlen)) in a free slot.
Returns 1 if queued, 0 if the queue is full (caller should fall
back to the drop-on-miss semantics).

    inet_queue_pending_udp(owner_tid: int, dst_ip: u32, dst_port: int, src_port: int, data: *byte, dlen: int) -> int
        if dlen > INET_PENDING_UDP_DATA_MAX
            return 0
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_PENDING_UDP_MAX
            if inet_pending_udp[i].in_use == 0
                inet_pending_udp[i].owner_tid = owner_tid
                inet_pending_udp[i].dst_ip = dst_ip
                inet_pending_udp[i].dst_port = dst_port
                inet_pending_udp[i].src_port = src_port
                inet_pending_udp[i].dlen = dlen
                var j = 0
                while j < dlen
                    inet_pending_udp[i].data[j] = data[j]
                    j += 1
                inet_pending_udp[i].in_use = 1
                return 1
            i += 1
        0


Phase 2 chunk 3 — drop pending UDP slots owned by a dying tid.
Without this, datagrams parked waiting for ARP would be sent
on behalf of a dead process when the reply finally lands. The
queue is only 2 slots so even a single leaker can starve it.

    inet_pending_udp_cleanup_owner(tid: int)
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_PENDING_UDP_MAX
            if inet_pending_udp[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_pending_udp[i].owner_tid == tid
                    inet_pending_udp[i].in_use = 0
            i += 1


Count in-use pending UDP slots whose owner_tid matches `tid`.

    inet_pending_udp_count_owner(tid: int) -> int
        var n = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_PENDING_UDP_MAX
            if inet_pending_udp[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_pending_udp[i].owner_tid == tid
                    n += 1
            i += 1
        n


Selftest helper for INET_CMD_PID_EXIT_SELFTEST. Parks a fake
pending UDP slot owned by `tid`, runs cleanup, verifies the
slot freed.

    inet_pending_udp_cleanup_selftest(tid: int) -> int
        if inet_pending_udp_count_owner(tid) != 0
            return 1
        var pad: [1]byte
        pad[0] = byte(0)
        if inet_queue_pending_udp(tid, u32(0x01020304), 0xbeef, 0xcafe, &pad[0], 1) != 1
            return 2
        if inet_pending_udp_count_owner(tid) != 1
            return 3
        inet_pending_udp_cleanup_owner(tid)
        if inet_pending_udp_count_owner(tid) != 0
            return 4
        0


Called from `inet_arp_insert` after a (possibly new) entry for
`ip` lands. Iterates pending UDP slots and retransmits any whose
next-hop resolves through `ip`. An on-subnet peer matches its
own IP directly; an off-subnet peer matches the gateway IP the
cache just learned. `inet_send_udp` recurses here with a full
ARP cache — the lookup will hit and the frame is emitted.

    inet_arp_drain_pending(ip: u32)
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_PENDING_UDP_MAX
            if inet_pending_udp[i].in_use != 0
                val slot_ip = inet_pending_udp[i].dst_ip
                var matches = 0
                if slot_ip == ip
                    matches = 1
                else if inet_in_subnet(slot_ip) == 0
                    val gw = ipv4(INET_GW_IP_A, INET_GW_IP_B,
                        INET_GW_IP_C, INET_GW_IP_D)
                    if ip == gw
                        matches = 1
                if matches != 0
                    val dst_port = inet_pending_udp[i].dst_port
                    val src_port = inet_pending_udp[i].src_port
                    val dlen = inet_pending_udp[i].dlen
                    val tid = inet_pending_udp[i].owner_tid
                    var payload: [1472]byte
                    var k = 0
                    while k < dlen
                        payload[k] = inet_pending_udp[i].data[k]
                        k += 1
                    inet_pending_udp[i].in_use = 0
                    inet_send_udp(tid, slot_ip, dst_port, src_port,
                        payload, dlen)
            i += 1

        // Walk TCP sockets that previously failed to emit because
        // of an ARP miss.  Defined later in the file (after
        // `tcp_sockets`) so the analyzer's forward function
        // resolution carries us through the reference.
        inet_tcp_arp_drain(ip)

